---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import AtomSim from '../../simulators/atom/AtomSim.astro';
import { atomQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/atom';

const toc = [
  { id: 'particles', label: 'The three particles' },
  { id: 'numbers', label: 'Atomic and mass numbers' },
  { id: 'isotopes', label: 'Isotopes' },
  { id: 'ions', label: 'Ions' },
  { id: 'shells', label: 'Electron shells' },
  { id: 'energy', label: 'Energy levels and light' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world applications' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the model simplifies' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Flowers, P., Theopold, K., Langley, R., & Robinson, W. R. (2019). Chemistry 2e, Section 2.3: Atomic Structure and Symbolism. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-3-atomic-structure-and-symbolism' },
  { text: 'Flowers, P., et al. (2019). Chemistry 2e, Section 6.2: The Bohr Model. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-2-the-bohr-model' },
  { text: 'International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC). Periodic Table of the Elements and Isotopes.', url: 'https://iupac.org/what-we-do/periodic-table-of-elements/' },
  { text: 'National Nuclear Data Center, Brookhaven National Laboratory. NuDat — nuclear structure and decay data.', url: 'https://www.nndc.bnl.gov/nudat3/' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="chemistry"
  topic="atomic-structure"
  description="Interactive atom builder: add protons, neutrons and electrons to build the first 20 elements, and explore atomic number, mass number, isotopes, ions and electron shells."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={atomQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Everything around you — air, water, your own body — is made of atoms, and every atom is built from just three
      kinds of particles. Add and remove protons, neutrons and electrons to build any of the first 20 elements, and see
      how each particle changes the atom’s identity, mass and charge.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <AtomSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Use the <strong>+</strong> and <strong>−</strong> buttons to add or remove <strong>protons, neutrons and electrons</strong>, or choose an example.</li>
      <li>The <strong>element tile</strong> shows the symbol, atomic number (top left) and mass number (top right).</li>
      <li>Read the <strong>readouts</strong> to see whether you have a neutral atom or an ion, and whether the nucleus is stable.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Excite an electron</strong> to watch an electron jump to a higher shell and emit light as it falls back.</li>
      <li>Click a particle to identify it. <strong>Start/Pause</strong> controls the electron motion; <strong>Reset</strong> returns to carbon-12.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="particles">The three particles</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Particle</th><th>Charge</th><th>Relative mass</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Proton</strong></td><td>+1</td><td>1</td><td>Nucleus</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Neutron</strong></td><td>0</td><td>1 (very slightly more)</td><td>Nucleus</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Electron</strong></td><td>−1</td><td>About 1/1836</td><td>Shells around the nucleus</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The <strong>nucleus</strong> contains almost all of an atom’s mass but is tiny — roughly 100,000 times smaller than
    the atom itself. The rest of the atom is the region where electrons are found.
  </p>

  <h2 id="numbers">Atomic and mass numbers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The <strong>atomic number (Z)</strong> is the number of protons. It defines the element: every carbon atom has 6 protons, every oxygen atom has 8.</li>
    <li>The <strong>mass number (A)</strong> is the number of protons plus neutrons.</li>
  </ul>
  <code class="formula">A = Z + N        number of neutrons N = A − Z</code>
  <p>Chemists write an atom as a symbol with A at the top left and Z at the bottom left, for example ¹²₆C.</p>

  <h2 id="isotopes">Isotopes</h2>
  <p>
    Atoms of the same element can have different numbers of neutrons. These versions are called
    <strong>isotopes</strong>. They behave almost identically in chemical reactions because they have the same electrons,
    but their masses differ. Some combinations of protons and neutrons are <strong>stable</strong>; others are
    <strong>radioactive</strong> and eventually decay. Carbon-12 and carbon-13 are stable, while carbon-14 is
    radioactive with a half-life of about 5,700 years. Too many or too few neutrons and a nucleus cannot hold together at all.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ions">Ions</h2>
  <p>
    In a neutral atom, the number of electrons equals the number of protons, so the charges cancel. If an atom
    <strong>loses</strong> electrons it becomes a positive ion (<strong>cation</strong>), such as Na⁺. If it
    <strong>gains</strong> electrons it becomes a negative ion (<strong>anion</strong>), such as Cl⁻.
  </p>
  <code class="formula">charge = protons − electrons</code>

  <h2 id="shells">Electron shells</h2>
  <p>
    In the simple shell model, electrons occupy shells at increasing distances from the nucleus. For the first 20
    elements, the shells fill in the pattern <strong>2, 8, 8, 2</strong>. The electrons in the outermost shell, called
    <strong>valence electrons</strong>, decide how an element reacts. Atoms with full outer shells, such as neon and argon,
    are very unreactive (noble gases). Sodium, with one valence electron, readily loses it; chlorine, one short of a full
    shell, readily gains one. That is why sodium and chlorine combine to form sodium chloride.
  </p>

  <h2 id="energy">Energy levels and light</h2>
  <p>
    Each shell corresponds to an <strong>energy level</strong>. An electron can absorb exactly the right amount of energy
    to jump to a higher level, then fall back and release that energy as a <strong>photon</strong> of light. Because each
    element has its own pattern of energy levels, each produces its own colours — sodium streetlights glow orange-yellow,
    and astronomers identify elements in stars from their light.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Element</dt>
    <dd>A substance made of atoms that all have the same number of protons.</dd>
    <dt>Isotope</dt>
    <dd>Atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons.</dd>
    <dt>Ion</dt>
    <dd>An atom or group of atoms with an electric charge because electrons were gained or lost.</dd>
    <dt>Valence electrons</dt>
    <dd>Electrons in the outermost shell, involved in chemical bonding.</dd>
    <dt>Radioactive decay</dt>
    <dd>The spontaneous change of an unstable nucleus, releasing radiation.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world applications</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Radiocarbon dating</strong> uses carbon-14 to find the age of wood, bone and other once-living material up to about 50,000 years old.</li>
    <li><strong>Medical imaging</strong> uses radioactive isotopes such as technetium-99m as tracers (an educational example — see our <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Nerves and muscles</strong> work using ions such as Na⁺, K⁺ and Ca²⁺ moving across cell membranes.</li>
    <li><strong>Fireworks and neon signs</strong> get their colours from electrons dropping between energy levels.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Electrons orbit like planets.”</strong> The Bohr picture is a useful model, but electrons are better described as clouds of probability called orbitals.</li>
    <li><strong>“Changing neutrons changes the element.”</strong> Only protons decide the element; neutrons give different isotopes.</li>
    <li><strong>“Ions are different elements.”</strong> Na⁺ is still sodium — only its electron count changed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the model simplifies</h2>
  <p>
    The Bohr model, proposed in 1913, places electrons on circular shells. Modern quantum mechanics replaces these with
    orbitals of different shapes, and from potassium onward the filling order becomes more complex. The nucleus here is
    drawn hugely enlarged, and stability is simplified to three categories using the known stable isotopes of the first
    20 elements.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Protons set the element, neutrons set the isotope, electrons set the charge.</li>
    <li>Mass number = protons + neutrons.</li>
    <li>Ions form when atoms gain or lose electrons.</li>
    <li>Valence electrons in the outer shell control chemical behaviour.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
